arguments.content:
## Fixed — the boot/slash download output now rides the `log` seam

`_downloadVerifiedArchive` takes the seam and passes it down:

```dart
// bin/self_manage.dart
final outcome = await downloadArchiveBody(
  client,
  Uri.parse(url),
  // The same seam every other message on this path uses (review r3):
  // the boot hook logs the body phase to fa.log, the /update slash
  // command renders it in-session — never raw stdout.
  log: log,
  ...
);
```

- `applyUpdate` already computes `final log = logLine ?? logUpdateLine;`; it now threads `log: log` into `_downloadVerifiedArchive` (bin/self_manage.dart:1346), whose signature gained `required void Function(String message) log` (bin/self_manage.dart:1516) and forwards it to `downloadArchiveBody` (bin/self_manage.dart:1525).
- All AC2/AC3 output — `downloading… 4.2/9.5 MB`, `connection lost … resuming (attempt n/m)…`, `downloaded … (… KB/s)` — lands in `fa.log` on the boot hook and renders through the session seam on `/update`, exactly like the check/swap/restart messages. The interactive `fa update` path (`_binaryUpdate`, `log: _say`) is untouched.

TDD: new test `applyUpdate > review r3: the download output rides the logLine seam, not stdout` (test/cli/self_manage_test.dart:1091) captures `logLine` on the happy boot-update path and asserts the final `downloaded …` line arrives through the seam — it failed on the pre-fix head (the line went to raw stdout) and passes now.

## Fixed — bytes-complete-despite-tail-error guard added

The guard is in `_drain`'s catch (bin/self_manage.dart:386–393), ahead of the resume policy:

```dart
} catch (error) {
  received = builder.length;
  final total = totalBytes;
  if (total != null && received >= total) {
    // Every promised byte is already in the builder (review r5): a
    // tail error — a reset racing the final byte, a proxy that
    // over-sends then dies — must not waste the complete archive on
    // a resume a well-behaved server answers 416.
    return (null, false);
  }
  ...
}
```

`(null, false)` is the clean-success return, so `run` proceeds to its normal completeness check (`received < total` is false) and reports success — the reassembled bytes go through the same SHA256SUMS provenance check as any other completion, and no `Range: bytes=<received>-` request is ever sent. A tail error below the promised byte count still takes the bounded resume path, fail-closed as before.

TDD: new test `review r5: a stream error after the promised bytes still completes` (test/cli/self_manage_test.dart:2431) — the body delivers every promised byte, then errors; the mock server answers 416 to any follow-up request and counts requests. Pre-fix the outcome was the `http(416)` failure (RED); post-fix it is success with byte-identical content and exactly 1 request (GREEN).

## Fixed — both suggested shapes, combined

`_drainBody` (bin/self_manage.dart:185–246) now uses the nullable field **and** unwinds a synchronous `listen` throw through `fail`:

```dart
StreamSubscription<List<int>>? subscription;   // was `late final`
...
void fail(Object error) {
  if (completer.isCompleted) return;
  stallTimer?.cancel();
  totalTimer?.cancel();
  subscription?.cancel();
  completer.completeError(error);
}
...
try {
  totalTimer = Timer(totalTimeout, () { fail(TimeoutException(...)); });
  armStall();
  subscription = stream.listen(..., cancelOnError: true);
} on Object catch (error) {
  // A synchronous `listen` throw (review r6) must unwind through the
  // same fail path: the armed timers are cancelled and the error
  // becomes the drain failure, not a stray timer firing later.
  fail(error);
}
return completer.future;
```

The nullable field alone would still leave an unhandled future error (the completer's future is returned only after the sync throw, so nobody listens to its `completeError`) — the try/catch is what makes the unwind deterministic: timers cancelled, error becomes the drain failure, `_drain`'s catch turns it into the bounded resume-or-transport outcome.

TDD: new test `review r6: a synchronous listen throw leaves no armed timer behind` (test/cli/self_manage_test.dart:2472) with a stream whose `listen` throws `StateError` synchronously (`_SyncThrowStream`), `stallTimeout: 50ms`, `maxResumeAttempts: 0`, then sleeps past the armed stall timer. Pre-fix the timer's late fire read the unassigned `late final` and crashed the zone with `LateInitializationError` (RED); post-fix the outcome is a clean transport failure naming the error and nothing fires (GREEN).

## Fixed — the class doc now points at the public contract

Took exactly the suggested shape — the nine-bullet contract lives only on the public `downloadArchiveBody` (bin/self_manage.dart:451–472, the source of truth callers and docs read), and `final class _BodyDownload` (bin/self_manage.dart:250–259) keeps only its own concern:

```dart
